Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for turning the empty “page background” that you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are part of the **core MapLibre GL JS API** – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1. Prerequisites (what you already have)

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any style you like
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,               // you already set this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true         // important for smooth terrain shading
});

// 2️⃣  Add the DEM source (raster-dem) and enable terrain
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // ← your DEM tileset
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 12
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
```

If you can already see the 3-D relief, the code above (or its equivalent) is already in place.

---

## 2. Add a **sky** object

### 2-a. Using the **style JSON** (static style)

If you prefer to keep everything in the style file, add a top-level `sky` object:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [ … ],
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#199EF3",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5,
    "fog-color": "rgba(135,206,235,0.6)",   // light blue with some alpha
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5
  }
}
```

*All keys are **root-level** (they are **not** a layer type).*

### 2-b. Using the **runtime API** (dynamic, works after the map is loaded)

If you already have a map instance, call `map.setSky()` **after** the `load` event:

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 3️⃣  Add the sky
  map.setSky({
    // colour of the zenith (the “deep” part of the sky)
    'sky-color': '#199EF3',               // a calm blue

    // how quickly the colour blends into the horizon colour
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,              // 0 = hard line, 1 = very smooth

    // colour of the horizon line itself
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',           // white / light-gray

    // how the horizon colour mixes with the fog colour
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,

    // colour of the fog / haze that appears where the ground meets the sky
    // you can use rgba() to control opacity (alpha)
    'fog-color': 'rgba(135,206,235,0.6)', // light-sky-blue, 60 % opaque

    // how quickly the fog fades into the ground (0 = no fog, 1 = thick fog)
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
  });
});
```

> **Why use `setSky` instead of a layer?**  
> In MapLibre the sky is **not a layer** – it lives at the root of the style. Trying to add a layer with `type: "sky"` will be ignored silently.

---

## 3. What each property does (quick cheat-sheet)

| Property | What it controls | Typical values |
|----------|------------------|----------------|
| `sky-color` | Colour of the sky at the zenith (top). | Any CSS colour, e.g. `#199EF3` (mid-day blue). |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | Smoothness of the transition from `sky-color` to `horizon-color`. | `0` = hard line, `1` = very gradual. |
| `horizon-color` | Colour of the line where sky meets ground. | Usually a light colour (`#fff`, `#f0f0f0`). |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | How the horizon colour mixes with the fog colour. | `0–1`. |
| `fog-color` | Colour (and opacity) of the haze that appears near the horizon. | `rgba(r,g,b,alpha)` – lower alpha = thinner haze. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | How quickly the fog fades into the terrain. | `0` = no fog, `1` = thick fog that reaches the ground. |

Play with the numbers until the sky looks the way you want. A common “soft-haze” combo is:

```js
{
  'sky-color': '#6ab7ff',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.6,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
  'fog-color': 'rgba(200,225,255,0.45)',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.4
}
```

---

## 4. Full Minimal Working Example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain with sky & fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 12
        },
        // you can add an imagery source here if you want
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 19
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'sat',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'sat'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // Enable terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // Add the sky + fog
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': 'rgba(135,206,235,0.6)',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map (or use the mouse wheel while holding **Shift**), and you’ll see:

* the 3-D terrain,
* a blue sky that fades into a white horizon,
* a light blue haze that meets the ground – no more blank page background.

---

## 5. Common Gotchas & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **The sky stays black / empty** | `map.setSky()` was called **before** the `load` event, or the map has no terrain (`setTerrain` not called). | Call `setSky` **after** `map.on('load')` and make sure `setTerrain` is active. |
| **Fog never appears** | `fog-color` is fully opaque (`rgba(...,1)`) **or** `fog-ground-blend` is `0`. | Use an alpha < 1 (e.g. `0.5`) and set `fog-ground-blend` > 0. |
| **Sky colour looks washed out** | The map’s background layer is covering the sky (e.g. a `background` layer with a solid colour). | Move the `background` layer **below** the sky (it’s automatically below, but if you added a custom background layer with a high `z-index`, lower its `layer` order). |
| **Horizon line is too sharp** | `sky-horizon-blend` is `0`. | Increase it to `0.3–0.7`. |
| **Fog looks like a solid band** | `horizon-fog-blend` is `0` (no blending) or `fog-color` alpha is too high. | Raise `horizon-fog-blend` and lower the alpha. |
| **Performance drops a lot** | Very high `exaggeration` + high `pitch` + many draped raster layers. | Keep exaggeration modest (1–2), limit the number of raster layers, and enable `antialias: true` only if you need smooth shading. |

---

## 6. TL;DR – The Minimal Commands You Need

```js
// after map has loaded and terrain is on:
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': 'rgba(135,206,235,0.6)',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

That’s it! The sky and haze will now fill the space above the horizon, giving your 3-D terrain a realistic backdrop. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for turning the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a proper sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native to **MapLibre GL JS** – no extra plugins are needed.

---

## 1️⃣  What you already have (the good part)

```js
// terrain source
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // …optional: encoding, maxzoom, etc.
});

// turn the DEM into a 3-D surface
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

// tilt the view so you can see the 3-D shape
map.easeTo({ pitch: 70, bearing: 0 });
```

That gives you the **relief** you like.  
What you’re missing is the **sky** object that lives at the *root* of the style (it is **not** a layer).

---

## 2️⃣  Add a sky object

You can add the sky **once the map has finished loading** (or you can bake it into the style JSON).  
Both approaches are shown; pick the one you prefer.

### 2A️⃣  Dynamically, after `map.load`  

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣  Make sure terrain is on (already done above)
  // 2️⃣  Add the sky
  map.setSky({
    // overall colour of the dome (think “day-sky”)
    'sky-color': '#88ccee',               // light blue

    // how quickly the colour fades to the horizon colour
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,              // 0 = no blend, 1 = full blend

    // colour of the very edge of the dome (the “horizon line”)
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',           // crisp white

    // how the horizon colour mixes with the fog colour
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,              // 0 = no fog at horizon, 1 = full fog

    // colour of the atmospheric haze that appears near the ground
    'fog-color': '#cceeff',                // a very pale blue-white

    // how quickly the fog fades into the ground (0 = no fog, 1 = all fog)
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.2,               // a light veil

    // optional: extra “atmosphere” blend for a subtle glow
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.5                // 0 = none, 1 = full atmosphere
  });

  // (optional) make the background layer transparent so the sky can be seen
  const bg = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'background');
  if (bg) {
    map.setPaintProperty(bg.id, 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
  }
});
```

### 2B️⃣  Bake it into the style JSON  

If you prefer to keep everything in the style file, add a top-level `sky` object:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [ … ],
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#88ccee",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.3,
    "fog-color": "#cceeff",
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.2,
    "atmosphere-blend": 0.5
  }
}
```

Then load the style as usual:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://my.server.com/style-with-sky.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 5,
  pitch: 70
});
```

---

## 3️⃣  Why the sky appears *above* the horizon only after you add it

* The sky is **not a layer** – it lives at the root of the style, just like `terrain`.  
* If no sky object exists, the map falls back to the **background colour** (usually the page’s default, e.g. white).  
* Adding the sky replaces that fallback with a rendered dome that automatically follows the camera’s pitch and bearing.

---

## 4️⃣  Fine-tuning the look

| Property | What it does | Typical range |
|----------|--------------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | Base colour of the dome. | Light blues for day, deeper blues for evening. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | How fast the colour fades to the horizon colour. | 0 – 1 (0 = sharp line, 1 = very smooth). |
| `horizon-color` | Colour of the very edge of the dome. | Usually white or a pale tint. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | Mix of horizon colour with fog colour. | 0 – 1 (higher = more fog at the horizon). |
| `fog-color` | Colour of the atmospheric haze that meets the ground. | Light blues, greys, or even a sunset pink. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | How quickly the fog fades into the terrain. | 0 – 1 (lower = thin veil, higher = thick fog). |
| `atmosphere-blend` | Extra “glow” that simulates scattering. | 0 – 1 (optional, often 0.3-0.6). |

Play with the numbers in the browser console until the sky looks the way you want. The changes are **instantaneous** – no reload required.

---

## 5️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Sky still missing** | `map.setSky()` called **before** the map’s `load` event. | Move the call into `map.on('load', …)` or after `map.once('idle', …)`. |
| **Background colour still shows** | A `background` layer with an opaque colour sits on top of the sky. | Set its `background-color` to transparent (`rgba(0,0,0,0)`) or remove the layer. |
| **Fog never appears** | Terrain is not enabled or `exaggeration` is 0. | Ensure `map.setTerrain()` is active; fog only renders when a DEM is present. |
| **Sky looks flat (no curvature)** | Using an old MapLibre version (< 2.4) that lacks the sky implementation. | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (≥ 2.4). |
| **Colors look washed-out** | `sky-horizon-blend` or `horizon-fog-blend` set too high. | Reduce the blend values; keep them ≤ 0.6 for a natural look. |

---

## 6️⃣  Full minimal example (copy-paste ready)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain with sky & fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
          tileSize: 256,
          // encoding: 'terrarium' // if needed
        },
        // add any raster or vector sources you want draped over the terrain here
      },
      layers: [
        // example raster imagery draped over the DEM
        {
          id: 'sat',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'terrain-dem',
          paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.7 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 5,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true   // required for smooth terrain rendering
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣  Enable terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // 2️⃣  Add sky + fog
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#88ccee',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,
      'fog-color': '#cceeff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.2,
      'atmosphere-blend': 0.5
    });

    // 3️⃣  Make background transparent (optional but often useful)
    const bg = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'background');
    if (bg) map.setPaintProperty(bg.id, 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, tilt the map, and you should see:

* A **curved sky dome** that follows the camera.
* A **soft haze** that thickens near the ground and fades toward the horizon.
* The terrain rendered beneath it, with the same relief you already had.

