Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe that turns the “blank page” you see above the horizon into a proper sky with a gentle haze (fog) that follows the ground.  
It works with any MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.5 (the version that introduced the `sky` object) and assumes you already have:

* a `raster-dem` source called **`terrain-dem`** (or whatever you named it)  
* `map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: …})` turned on  
* the map pitched (e.g. `pitch: 70`) so the terrain is visible

---

## 1️⃣  Add the sky **after** the map and terrain are ready

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map (any style you like)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [13.4, 52.5],           // lng, lat
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,                     // you already have this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true                // recommended for 3-D
});

// 2️⃣  When the style is loaded, add the DEM source & terrain
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // ---- DEM source -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json', // ← your DEM tiles
    tileSize: 256,
    // optional: encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' (match your source)
  });

  // ---- Enable 3-D terrain -----------------------------------------
  map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.0});

  // ---- **Add the sky** --------------------------------------------
  // This must be called *after* terrain is on, otherwise the sky
  // will not be rendered.
  map.setSky({
    // Overall sky colour (day-time blue)
    'sky-color': '#87CEEB',

    // Colour of the horizon line – usually a lighter version of the sky
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

    // How quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon (0-1)
    // 0 = abrupt line, 1 = very smooth gradient
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

    // Fog (haze) that appears where the ground meets the sky.
    // It only works when a terrain source is active.
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // a soft, slightly desaturated blue
    // Blend of the fog colour into the horizon (0-1)
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
    // Blend of the fog colour into the ground (0-1)
    // Larger values make the fog reach farther down the slopes.
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
  });

  // (Optional) If you want a subtle atmospheric glow you can also
  // tweak the sky-horizon-blend or fog-ground-blend values.
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| **`sky-color`** | any CSS colour | Base colour of the sky dome. |
| **`horizon-color`** | any CSS colour | Colour of the thin line where sky meets ground. |
| **`sky-horizon-blend`** | `0 … 1` | Controls how smoothly the sky colour fades into the horizon. |
| **`fog-color`** | any CSS colour (usually a muted blue/gray) | Colour of the atmospheric haze that “fills” the space above the terrain. |
| **`horizon-fog-blend`** | `0 … 1` | How much the fog colour mixes into the horizon line. |
| **`fog-ground-blend`** | `0 … 1` | How far the fog extends down the slopes. Larger → thicker haze near the ground. |

> **Tip:** Play with the three blend values together. A common “soft-haze” look is `sky-horizon-blend: 0.6`, `horizon-fog-blend: 0.4`, `fog-ground-blend: 0.3`. Adjust the colours until you get the mood you want (sunset, overcast, etc.).

---

## 2️⃣  Why the sky was missing before

| Symptom | Typical cause |
|---------|----------------|
| **Blank page background above the horizon** | No `sky` object defined, or it was added **before** `setTerrain` (the sky only renders when a terrain source is active). |
| **Fog does nothing** | Either `setTerrain` is off, or you used a `sky` **layer** (MapLibre never had a `type: "sky"` layer). |
| **`sky-type`, `sky-atmosphere-sun`, …** | Those are Mapbox-only properties; MapLibre ignores them. Use only the properties listed above. |

