## 5️⃣  Quick checklist (so you don’t miss anything)

1. **MapLibre version ≥ 5.5** (sky was added in 5.5).  
2. **`map.setTerrain()`** is called and the source is a valid `raster-dem`.  
3. **Map pitch > 0** (you already have 70°).  
4. **`map.setSky()`** is called *after* terrain is enabled (inside `style.load` or after `setTerrain`).  
5. Use only the **sky properties listed above** – any `sky-type`, `sky-atmosphere-*` etc. are ignored.  

If you follow the code snippet and checklist, the blank background will be replaced by a proper sky with haze that respects the terrain’s shape. Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a proper sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native MapLibre GL JS – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1️⃣  Prerequisites – you already have these

```js
// 1️⃣  A MapLibre map instance
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any style you like
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,               // you already set this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true         // important for smooth terrain rendering
});

// 2️⃣  A raster-dem source (the same one you use for terrain)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 12
});

// 3️⃣  3-D terrain turned on
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
```

If you can already see the hills and valleys, the three lines above are working.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a **sky** object

The sky is **not a layer** – it lives at the root of the style (or can be added later with `map.setSky`).  
When a sky object is present, MapLibre draws it *behind* every layer (including the background layer).  
If you have a background layer that is fully opaque (`background-color: #fff`), it will hide the sky, so either:

* remove that background layer, **or**
* make its color transparent (`background-color: rgba(0,0,0,0)`).

### 2.1  Quick-and-dirty “add-sky-after-load” version

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣  (optional) make the background transparent so the sky can be seen
  const bg = map.getLayer('background');
  if (bg) {
    map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
  }

  // 2️⃣  Add the sky
  map.setSky({
    // overall sky colour – a light blue works well for a clear day
    'sky-color': '#87CEEB',

    // colour of the horizon line – usually a little brighter / whiter
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

    // how quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon (0-1)
    // 0 = abrupt line, 1 = very smooth transition
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

    // fog (haze) that appears where the terrain meets the sky
    // fog-color is the colour of the haze; you can use a very light blue
    // or even a subtle gray for “mist”.
    'fog-color': '#cce6ff',

    // how much the fog colour blends into the horizon colour (0-1)
    // larger values give a thicker haze near the horizon
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,

    // how much the fog colour blends into the ground colour (0-1)
    // 0 = fog stops at the horizon, 1 = fog climbs up the slopes
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
  });
});
```

That’s it – the sky will appear automatically once the map is pitched > 0 and terrain is on.

### 2.2  Declaring the sky **in the style JSON** (if you prefer a static style)

If you are authoring a style file (or using a style editor), add a top-level `sky` object:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "terrain-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json",
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 12
    },
    "...": "..."
  },

  "terrain": {
    "source": "terrain-dem",
    "exaggeration": 1
  },

  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#87CEEB",
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "fog-color": "#cce6ff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.4,
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.3
  },

  "layers": [
    // your other layers (hillshade, raster, vector, etc.)
  ]
}
```

When you load this style the sky is already there – no extra JavaScript needed.

---

## 3️⃣  Fine-tuning the look

| Property | What it does | Typical range |
|----------|--------------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | Base colour of the sky dome. | Light blues (`#87CEEB`) → deeper blues (`#4a90e2`). |
| `horizon-color` | Colour exactly at the horizon line. | Usually a little brighter than `sky-color`. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | Smoothness of the colour transition from sky to horizon. | `0.3 – 0.7` (higher = softer). |
| `fog-color` | Colour of the haze that blends with the terrain. | Light blue, pale gray, or even a warm pink for sunrise. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | How far the fog reaches *up* from the horizon. | `0.2 – 0.6`. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | How far the fog climbs *up* the slopes. | `0.2 – 0.5`. |

Play with the numbers in the browser console until you get the atmosphere you like.  
A good starting point for a “clear-day with a thin haze” is the values shown in the code above.

---

## 4️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Sky never appears** (you still see the plain page background) | • No sky object in the style **or** you called `map.setSky` **before** the map finished loading. <br>• An opaque background layer (`background-color: #fff`) sits on top of the sky. | • Call `map.setSky` inside a `map.on('load', …)` handler (or after the style is fully applied). <br>• Make the background transparent or remove it. |
| **Fog looks like a solid band at the horizon** | `horizon-fog-blend` or `fog-ground-blend` set to `0`. | Increase those blend values (e.g., `0.4`). |
| **Sky looks too dark / night-like** | `sky-color` is too dark or the default `sky-color` (`#000000`) is being used. | Choose a lighter colour (`#87CEEB` or similar). |
| **Fog disappears when you zoom out** | The fog is rendered in screen space; at very low zoom the terrain mesh is tiny, so the fog effect is less noticeable. | This is expected – you can increase `fog-ground-blend` to make the haze more pronounced at low zoom. |
| **Performance drops dramatically** | You have many heavy layers (e.g., multiple hillshade passes) **and** a sky with fog. | Keep the elevation stack small: one `raster-dem` source, one `hillshade` (or `color-relief`) layer, and the sky. Use `hillshade-method: "multidirectional"` instead of stacking several hillshade layers. |

---

