## 6️⃣  TL;DR checklist

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})`).  
2. **Pitch the map** (`pitch > 0`).  
3. **Add a sky object** – either in the style JSON (`"sky": { … }`) **or** via `map.setSky({ … })` after the map loads.  
4. **Make the background transparent** (or remove it) so the sky can be seen.  
5. Adjust the six sky properties (`sky-color`, `horizon-color`, `sky-horizon-blend`, `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) until the visual matches what you want.

That’s all you need to replace the blank page background with a proper sky and atmospheric haze in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a proper sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native MapLibre GL JS – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1️⃣  Prerequisites – you already have these

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any style you like
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,               // you already set this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true          // important for smooth terrain rendering
});

// 2️⃣  Add the DEM source (raster-dem) and enable terrain
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',   // ← your DEM tileset
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 12
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
```

If the code above works you already see the 3-D surface, but the area above the horizon is just the browser’s white (or whatever `background-color` you gave the page).  
That’s because **MapLibre draws the sky as a *root-level* object, not as a layer**.  
You need to add a `sky` object (or call `map.setSky()`) and give it some fog parameters.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a sky object (or call `setSky`)

You can do it **once the map has finished loading** – either in the `style.load` event or after you call `map.setTerrain()`.

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  SKY + FOG (haze) configuration
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.setSky({
    // The colour of the sky itself (looks like a clear blue)
    'sky-color': '#87CEEB',               // light sky blue

    // How the sky colour blends into the horizon line
    // 0 = hard line, 1 = very smooth transition
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

    // Colour of the horizon line (usually a lighter version of sky)
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

    // How the horizon colour blends into the fog (see below)
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // FOG (the haze that makes distant terrain fade)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Colour of the fog – usually a desaturated version of the sky.
    // It is blended with the terrain based on distance from the camera.
    'fog-color': '#cce0ff',

    // How strongly the fog colour mixes with the ground colour.
    // 0 = no fog, 1 = full fog at the ground.
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.4
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // OPTIONAL: make the background layer transparent
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // If your style contains a `background` layer with an opaque colour,
  // the sky will be hidden.  Either remove that layer or set its colour
  // to transparent.
  const bg = map.getLayer('background');
  if (bg) {
    map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
  }
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | What it controls | Typical values |
|----------|------------------|----------------|
| **sky-color** | Base colour of the sky dome. | Any CSS colour (`#87CEEB`, `rgb(135,206,235)`, etc.) |
| **sky-horizon-blend** | Softness of the transition from sky to horizon. | `0.3-0.7` works well |
| **horizon-color** | Colour of the thin line where sky meets ground. | Usually a lighter version of `sky-color` (`#ffffff` works for most) |
| **horizon-fog-blend** | How quickly the horizon colour fades into the fog. | `0.2-0.5` |
| **fog-color** | Colour of the atmospheric haze that fades distant terrain. | Slightly desaturated sky colour (`#cce0ff`) |
| **fog-ground-blend** | Strength of the fog at the ground level. | `0.2-0.5` (higher = thicker haze) |

> **Tip:** Play with the two “blend” numbers until the horizon looks natural for your data. Small changes (0.05-0.1) can make a big visual difference.

---

## 3️⃣  Verify that the sky is actually being drawn

1. **Pitch the map** (you already have `pitch: 70`).  
2. **Zoom out a bit** – you should now see a blue dome above the terrain, with a faint white horizon line and a gentle fade-out of distant mountains into the fog colour.  
3. If you still see a plain white/grey background, double-check:
   * The `background` layer (if any) is transparent or removed.  
   * You called `map.setSky()` **after** the map’s `load` event (or after `setTerrain`).  
   * You are using a recent version of MapLibre GL JS (≥ 5.5). Older versions do not have the `sky` object.

---

## 4️⃣  Full Minimal Example (copy-paste ready)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Terrain + Sky + Fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [13.4, 52.5],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // Enable 3-D terrain
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // SKY + FOG
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,
      'fog-color': '#cce0ff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.4
    });

    // Make background transparent (optional, depends on your style)
    const bg = map.getLayer('background');
    if (bg) {
      map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map, and you should see a blue sky with a soft horizon and a faint haze that makes distant terrain fade away.

---

## 5️⃣  Common Gotchas & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Sky never appears (still white)** | A `background` layer with an opaque colour sits on top of the sky. | Remove the layer or set its `background-color` to transparent (`rgba(0,0,0,0)`). |
| **Fog looks too strong / everything disappears** | `fog-ground-blend` is too high or `fog-color` is too dark. | Lower `fog-ground-blend` (e.g., `0.2`) or use a lighter `fog-color`. |
| **Horizon line is a hard black edge** | `sky-horizon-blend` is `0` (hard line) or `horizon-color` is dark. | Increase `sky-horizon-blend` to `0.4-0.6` and use a light `horizon-color`. |
| **No sky at all, even after `setSky`** | Using a MapLibre version older than 5.5 (sky not yet implemented). | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (≥ 5.5). |
| **Sky disappears after changing the style** | `setSky` was called before the new style finished loading. | Re-run `map.setSky(...)` inside the new style’s `load` event. |

---

## 6️⃣  Quick Reference – Sky API

| Method / Property | Description |
|-------------------|-------------|
| `map.setSky(skyObject)` | Adds or replaces the root-level `sky` object. |
| `map.getSky()` | Returns the current sky configuration (or `null`). |
| `skyObject` fields | `sky-color`, `sky-horizon-blend`, `horizon-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-color`, `fog-ground-blend` |
| **No layer type** | There is **no** `type: "sky"` layer – the sky lives outside the layer stack. |
| **Works only with terrain** | Fog (`fog-color`, `fog-ground-blend`) is ignored unless `map.setTerrain()` is active. |

---

### 🎉 You’re done!

You now have:

* **3-D terrain** (already working)  
* **A realistic sky dome** that appears above the horizon  
* **Gentle atmospheric haze** that makes distant mountains fade naturally  

Feel free to tweak the colour palette to match the mood of your map (sunset, night, etc.) – just change the hex values and blend factors. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for turning the empty “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native MapLibre GL JS – no extra plugins are required.

---

## 1️⃣  Prerequisites – you already have these

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any style you like
  center: [13.4, 52.5],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 70,               // you already set this
  bearing: 0,
  antialias: true          // important for smooth terrain shading
});

// 2️⃣  Add the DEM source (raster-dem) and enable terrain
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',   // ← your DEM tileset
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 12,
  encoding: 'terrarium'   // or 'mapbox' – must match the source tiles
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
```

If you can already see the 3-D relief, the code above (or its equivalent) is already in place.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a **sky** object

The sky is **not a layer** – it lives at the root of the style (or can be added later with `map.setSky`).  
All sky-related properties are listed in the style spec under **sky**.

```js
// 2️⃣  Add a sky (you can call this any time after the map is ready)
map.on('load', () => {
  map.setSky({
    // overall colour of the dome (day-time blue)
    'sky-color': '#87CEEB',

    // colour of the horizon line – usually a lighter version of sky-color
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

    // how quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon (0-1)
    // 0 = hard line, 1 = very smooth transition
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

    // optional: add a faint fog that starts at the horizon and rises
    // (requires terrain to be on, otherwise the fog is ignored)
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',          // a light blue-gray
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,        // how quickly fog appears at the horizon
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.2,         // how quickly fog fades near the ground
    // you can also set a custom sun position with `sky-atmosphere-sun` (MapLibre ignores it,
    // but the property is kept for compatibility with Mapbox GL JS)
  });
});
```

### What each property does

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| **sky-color** | any CSS colour | Base colour of the dome (the “upper” sky). |
| **horizon-color** | any CSS colour | Colour of the line where sky meets terrain. |
| **sky-horizon-blend** | `0 … 1` | Controls the smoothness of the colour transition. |
| **fog-color** | any CSS colour | Colour of the atmospheric haze that starts at the horizon. |
| **horizon-fog-blend** | `0 … 1` | How quickly the fog becomes opaque at the horizon. |
| **fog-ground-blend** | `0 … 1` | How quickly the fog fades as it approaches the ground. |

> **Tip:** If you want a “sunset” feel, set `sky-color` to a warm orange (`#ffb347`) and `horizon-color` to a pink (`#ffcccb`). Adjust the blends to keep the transition smooth.

---

## 3️⃣  Make sure the **background** layer isn’t covering the sky

If you have a `background` layer in your style that is opaque (`background-color: #fff`), it will hide the sky.  
Either:

* Remove the background layer, **or**
* Set its opacity to `0` (or a semi-transparent colour).

```js
// Example: make the background transparent
map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
```

Most starter styles already have a transparent background, but it’s worth checking if you added a custom one.

---

## 4️⃣  (Optional) Add a **ground-level fog** for extra depth

If you want the fog to be denser close to the terrain (e.g., a misty valley), increase `fog-ground-blend`.  
If you want a very thin haze that only appears near the horizon, keep it low.

```js
// Thicker ground fog
map.setSky({
  ...map.getSky(),               // keep the existing sky settings
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

You can update any sky property later with `map.setSky()` again – the call replaces the whole sky object.

---

