## 6️⃣  Common Gotchas & How to Fix Them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Sky stays black** (or you still see the page background) | No `sky` object, or a solid `background` layer covering it. | Call `map.setSky()` *after* `map.on('load')` and ensure the background layer is transparent. |
| **Fog never appears** | Terrain is not enabled, or `fog-color` is the same as `sky-color`. | Make sure `map.setTerrain()` is called **before** `map.setSky()`. Use a contrasting fog colour (e.g., light gray/blue). |
| **Fog looks like a solid wall** | `horizon-fog-blend` set to `1` (full opacity at the horizon). | Reduce `horizon-fog-blend` to `0.2-0.5`. |
| **Sky colour looks washed out** | `sky-horizon-blend` too high (the horizon colour dominates). | Lower `sky-horizon-blend` (e.g., `0.3-0.5`). |
| **Performance drops on low-end devices** | Terrain + sky + fog are the most expensive rendering pass. | Reduce `exaggeration`, lower `maxzoom` of the DEM source, or disable sky on very low-end devices (`map.setSky(null)`). |

---

## 7️⃣  TL;DR – The essential code you need

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.2
});
```

Add the snippet **after** the map has loaded (or in a `map.on('load')` handler) and make sure any background layer is transparent. That’s all you need for a proper sky and atmospheric haze over your 3-D terrain. Happy mapping!

Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the blank “page background” you see above the horizon into a proper sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the code works with the current stable version of **MapLibre GL JS 5.x** (the same version that introduced `color-relief` and `hillshade-method`).

---

## 1️⃣  Why the sky is missing

* The map already has **3-D terrain** (`map.setTerrain(...)`) and a **pitch** (`70°`).  
* The sky is **not a layer** – it is a **root-level style object** (or a call to `map.setSky()`).  
* If you never add a sky object, the area above the terrain is simply the page’s background colour (or the colour of a `background` layer, if you added one).  

So the fix is: **add a sky object** (or call `map.setSky()`) and give it a fog colour that will blend with the terrain.

---

## 2️⃣  Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- DEM source (raster-dem) ---------------------------------
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          // Example DEM – replace with your own tilejson / URL
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 12
        },
        // ---- Imagery source (optional) -------------------------------
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 19
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // Base imagery (drawn *under* the terrain)
        { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' },

        // Optional: a background layer that fills the area *below* the horizon
        // (you can keep it or delete it – it does not affect the sky)
        {
          id: 'bg',
          type: 'background',
          paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0ff' }   // light-blue “ground-side” colour
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [ -122.447303, 37.753574 ], // any lon/lat you like
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,          // you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // required for terrain + sky
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Enable terrain (must be done *before* sky)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      exaggeration: 1.0          // tweak if you want more “mountain-ous” look
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the sky (the missing piece!)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // The colour of the sky *directly* above the viewer.
      // A light blue works well for a clear day.
      'sky-color': '#87CEEB',

      // The colour where the sky meets the horizon.
      // Usually a slightly paler or whiter tone.
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',

      // How quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon colour.
      // 0 = abrupt line, 1 = very smooth gradient.
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

      // Fog colour – this is the “haze” that appears where the terrain meets the sky.
      // It must be a colour that works with your sky-color; a soft blue-gray is common.
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',

      // How much the fog colour blends into the horizon colour.
      // 0 = no fog at the horizon, 1 = full fog right at the horizon.
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,

      // How quickly the fog fades as it approaches the ground.
      // 0 = fog reaches the ground, 1 = fog stops well above the ground.
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  (Optional) Add a hillshade or color-relief layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example hillshade – you can keep or remove it.
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **Create the map** | Loads a style that contains a `raster-dem` source (`terrain-dem`) and any base imagery you want. |
| **`map.setTerrain()`** | Turns the DEM into a 3-D mesh. The terrain must be enabled **before** you add a sky, otherwise the sky will be ignored. |
| **`map.setSky()`** | Adds a root-level sky object. The properties you see (`sky-color`, `horizon-color`, `sky-horizon-blend`, `fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`) are the **only sky-related properties MapLibre understands**. |
| **Optional hillshade** | Shows how you can still drape a hillshade (or a `color-relief` layer) on top of the terrain; it does not interfere with the sky. |

---

## 3️⃣  Important gotchas & troubleshooting

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **Sky never appears** | • `map.setTerrain()` was not called *or* was called **after** `map.setSky()`.<br>• `antialias: true` missing on map creation (terrain + sky need it). | Call `map.setTerrain()` first, then `map.setSky()`. Ensure `antialias: true`. |
| **Only a solid colour shows (no gradient)** | `sky-horizon-blend` set to `0` (or omitted, default is `0`). | Increase `sky-horizon-blend` (0.3-0.7 works well). |
| **Fog looks like a hard line at the horizon** | `horizon-fog-blend` is `0` (default). | Raise `horizon-fog-blend` to 0.3-0.6. |
| **Fog disappears when you zoom out** | Fog opacity is tied to the camera’s distance to the ground; at very low pitch or very high altitude the fog can become thin. | Keep a reasonable pitch (≥ 30°) and don’t zoom out beyond the DEM’s maxzoom (otherwise the terrain mesh disappears and fog is ignored). |
| **Background colour still shows above the sky** | You have a `background` layer with a high `z-index` (placed after the sky). | The sky is drawn **behind** all layers, but **above** the page background. A `background` layer only fills the area *under* the terrain; it never covers the sky. If you see a solid colour, you probably have a CSS `body { background: … }` that is visible because the map container is not covering the whole viewport. Make the map container `height:100%` and `width:100%`. |
| **Using Mapbox-only sky properties (`sky-type`, `sky-atmosphere-sun`, …)** | Those properties belong to Mapbox GL JS and are ignored by MapLibre. | Remove them; only the six properties listed above are respected. |

---

## 4️⃣  Fine-tuning the look

| Property | Typical range | Visual effect |
|----------|---------------|---------------|
| `sky-color` | `#87CEEB` (light sky) → `#001848` (twilight) | Overall colour of the sky dome. |
| `horizon-color` | `#ffffff` (bright) → `#6b8eae` (muted) | Colour where the sky meets the ground line. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | `0.3` – `0.7` | Smoothness of the sky-to-horizon transition. |
| `fog-color` | Light blue (`#c0d8ff`) → pale gray (`#d0d0d0`) | Colour of the haze that blends terrain into sky. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | `0.2` – `0.6` | How quickly the fog reaches the horizon. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | `0.2` – `0.5` | How far the fog extends down the slopes (larger = fog stays higher). |

Play with these values in the browser console:

```js
// Example: make a sunset sky
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#ffb347',
  'horizon-color': '#ffcc33',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.6,
  'fog-color': '#ff9e80',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.4
});
```

---

## 5️⃣  Full API reference (quick cheat-sheet)

| Method / Object | Description |
|-----------------|-------------|
| `map.setTerrain({ source: '<source-id>', exaggeration?: number })` | Enables 3-D terrain. Only `source` and `exaggeration` are valid. |
| `map.setSky({ 'sky-color'?, 'horizon-color'?, 'sky-horizon-blend'?, 'fog-color'?, 'horizon-fog-blend'?, 'fog-ground-blend'?, 'atmosphere-blend'? })` | Adds a sky object. All properties are **optional**; omitted ones fall back to defaults (`sky-color: #88ccee`, `horizon-color: #ffffff`, `sky-horizon-blend: 0.5`, `fog-color: #c0d8ff`, `horizon-fog-blend: 0.5`, `fog-ground-blend: 0.5`). |
| `map.getSky()` | Returns the current sky object (or `null` if none). |
| `map.queryTerrainElevation(lngLat)` | Returns the elevation (in metres) at a given coordinate **once terrain is on**. |
| `map.easeTo({ pitch, bearing, zoom, center, duration })` | Use to animate the camera after you add terrain/sky, e.g. `map.easeTo({ pitch: 70, duration: 2000 })`. |

---

## 6️⃣  TL;DR – Minimal code you can paste into an existing map

```js
// 1️⃣  Make sure terrain is on
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

// 2️⃣  Add the sky (must be after setTerrain)
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#87CEEB',
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.4,
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.3
});
```

That’s it—your map will now show a blue-gradient sky with a soft haze that meets the terrain, instead of the blank page background.

Enjoy the view! 🌄🚁
